\title{Finite Products of Moonshine-Type Series:\\Pole Aggregation, Weight Obstructions, and Character Reconstruction}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{18 July 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Motivated by the $194$ conjugacy classes of the Monster group and their McKay--Thompson series, we isolate the algebraic mechanisms governing finite products of normalized moonshine-type expansions. A normalized factor is written $T_c(q)=q^{-1}R_c(q)$ with $R_c(0)=1$. For a finite index set $S$, the product is exactly $q^{-|S|}\prod_{c\in S}R_c(q)$, so principal exponents add and the displayed leading term cannot cancel. Finite products of invariant functions remain invariant; consequently, weight-zero modular functions cannot acquire a nontrivial positive weight merely through multiplication. A compensator is the precise additional mechanism that transfers a nontrivial factor of automorphy. Finally, coefficient recovery is an injectivity problem for character evaluation. Injectivity recovers graded multiplicities degree by degree, while every collision yields distinct gradings with identical coefficient functions. We give algorithms, examples, applications to identifiability, and an analytic program for common-level modular products.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
The Monster group $\mathbb M$ is the largest sporadic finite simple group, of order
\[
2^{46}3^{20}5^9 7^6 11^2 13^3 17\,19\,23\,29\,31\,41\,47\,59\,71,
\]
approximately $8\times10^{53}$. Monstrous moonshine connects its graded representations to modular functions. The familiar entry point is
\[
j(\tau)-744=q^{-1}+196884q+21493760q^2+\cdots,
\qquad q=e^{2\pi i\tau}.
\]
More generally, a conjugacy class represented by $g$ has a McKay--Thompson series
\[
T_g(q)=\sum_{n\geq -1}a_n(g)q^n,
\]
whose coefficients are graded traces.

The Monster has $194$ conjugacy classes. This invites the proposal that multiplying the $194$ class-indexed functions creates one positive-weight modular form encoding the entire group. That proposal conflates four questions: how poles combine, how transformation laws combine, when coefficients determine representations, and whether all functions inhabit a common analytic setting. We settle the first three abstractly and formulate the fourth precisely.

Indexing matters. A product over $194$ classes has $194$ factors. A literal product over all group elements repeats each class function according to its class size and has $|\mathbb M|$ factors. Their pole orders are different. Weight also requires separate bookkeeping: a finite product of weight-zero functions remains weight zero on the common symmetry group.

\section{Normalized Laurent families}
Let $R$ be a commutative semiring, $R[q,q^{-1}]$ its Laurent polynomial ring, and $C$ an index set.

\begin{definition}[Normalized family]
A normalized family consists of Laurent polynomials $R_c(q)$ satisfying $R_c(0)=1$ and associated factors
\[
T_c(q)=q^{-1}R_c(q),\qquad c\in C.
\]
Here $R_c(0)$ denotes the coefficient of $q^0$. In the analytic interpretation, $R_c$ is regular at the infinite cusp.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Nonvanishing of regular factors]
If $R$ is nontrivial and $R_c(0)=1$, then $R_c\neq0$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The zero Laurent polynomial has coefficient $0$ at $q^0$, contradicting the normalization.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Normalized Product Factorization]
For every finite $S\subset C$,
\[
\prod_{c\in S}T_c(q)=q^{-|S|}\prod_{c\in S}R_c(q).
\]
If all $R_c$ are regular at $q=0$, the rightmost product has constant coefficient $1$. Hence the product has a pole of exact order $|S|$ and leading coefficient $1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Substitute $T_c=q^{-1}R_c$ and collect the $|S|$ monomials $q^{-1}$. Equivalently, induct on $S$; adjoining one factor changes $q^{-|S|}$ to $q^{-(|S|+1)}$. The constant coefficient of a product of regular factors is the product of their constant coefficients, namely $1$, so the displayed leading term cannot cancel.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Nonvanishing]
Over an integral domain, every finite normalized product is nonzero.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
The monomial is nonzero, each regular factor is nonzero by the lemma, and a domain has no zero divisors.
\end{proof}

\begin{example}
The symmetric group $S_3$ has three conjugacy classes. Any normalized class-indexed family satisfies
\[
T_1T_2T_3=q^{-3}R_1R_2R_3.
\]
The exponent records the three classes, not the six group elements.
\end{example}

\begin{corollary}[Monster class exponent]
A normalized family indexed once by each of the Monster's $194$ conjugacy classes has displayed product factor $q^{-194}$. If its regular factors are analytic in a compatible cusp coordinate, the pole at infinity has exact order $194$.
\end{corollary}

\section{Transformation laws and weight}
Let a transformation $\gamma$ act on a set $X$, and let functions take values in a commutative monoid.

\begin{definition}[Invariance]
A function $f$ is invariant under $\gamma$ if $f(\gamma x)=f(x)$ for every $x\in X$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Finite-Product Invariance]
If each $F_c:X\to A$, for $c$ in a finite set $S$, is invariant under $\gamma$, then $P(x)=\prod_{c\in S}F_c(x)$ is invariant under $\gamma$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Pointwise,
\[
P(\gamma x)=\prod_{c\in S}F_c(\gamma x)=\prod_{c\in S}F_c(x)=P(x).
\]
\end{proof}

If the factors are modular functions for groups $\Gamma_c$, the product is invariant under $\bigcap_c\Gamma_c$. Since the family is finite, no infinite-product convergence issue arises after meromorphy on a common domain is known.

\begin{theorem}[Weight Obstruction]
Let $K$ be a field and suppose
\[
P(\gamma x)=P(x),\qquad P(\gamma x)=J(x)P(x).
\]
At every $x$ for which $P(x)\neq0$, one has $J(x)=1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Equating the two values gives $J(x)P(x)=P(x)$; cancel the nonzero field element $P(x)$.
\end{proof}

Thus an invariant product cannot also carry a genuinely nontrivial factor such as $(c\tau+d)^k$ at nonzero points. The hypothesis $P(x)\neq0$ is necessary, because at a zero both laws say only $0=0$.

\begin{theorem}[Compensator Transfer]
Suppose $P(\gamma x)=P(x)$ and $A(\gamma x)=J(x)A(x)$. Then
\[
(AP)(\gamma x)=J(x)(AP)(x).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Multiply the two transformation laws and reassociate the factors.
\end{proof}

Therefore any desired nonzero weight must come from a compensator, multiplier system, or modified operation. If $A(q)=q^{-r}B(q)$ with $B(0)=1$, the compensator also adds principal exponent $-r$. Pole order and modular weight remain distinct invariants.

\section{Character encoding}
Let $\mathcal R$ index irreducible representations, $\mathcal C$ conjugacy classes, and $K$ a field. Define
\[
E:K^{\mathcal R}\longrightarrow K^{\mathcal C},
\qquad
(Em)(c)=\sum_{r\in\mathcal R}m(r)\chi_r(c).
\]
For a graded multiplicity sequence $m_n$, the observed coefficient function is $a_n=E(m_n)$.

\begin{definition}[Identifiability]
Character encoding is identifiable on a chosen multiplicity space if $E$ is injective there.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Graded Character Reconstruction]
If $E$ is injective and two graded assignments satisfy
\[
E(m_n)(c)=E(m'_n)(c)
\]
for every degree $n$ and class $c$, then $m_n=m'_n$ for every $n$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For fixed $n$, equality at all classes is equality of the two images under $E$. Injectivity gives $m_n=m'_n$. Apply this argument in every degree.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Collision Produces Indistinguishable Gradings]
If $u\neq v$ but $E(u)=E(v)$, then the constant gradings $m_n=u$ and $m'_n=v$ are distinct while satisfying $E(m_n)=E(m'_n)$ for every $n$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
They differ in degree zero, while the assumed collision makes their observations equal in each degree.
\end{proof}

For finite index sets, let $X_{c,r}=\chi_r(c)$. Then $a_n=Xm_n$, and injectivity is full column rank. A square invertible table yields $m_n=X^{-1}a_n$. A complete complex character table is invertible because irreducible characters form a basis of class functions. Restricted rows, truncation, or approximate observations require a fresh rank and stability analysis.

The family of classwise coefficients and its single product must not be confused. Product coefficients are convolutions that mix labels and degrees. Without an additional factorization theorem, one aggregate series need not recover its factors or the complete character table.

\section{Algorithms}
\subsection{Truncated normalized product}
Write
\[
R_c(q)=\sum_{k=0}^{N}r_{c,k}q^k+O(q^{N+1}),\qquad r_{c,0}=1.
\]
Initialize $p_0=1$ and $p_k=0$ for $k>0$. For each factor update
\[
p'_k=\sum_{i=0}^{k}p_i r_{c,k-i},\qquad 0\leq k\leq N.
\]
After $m$ factors, output $q^{-m}\sum_{k=0}^{N}p_kq^k$. Direct convolution costs $O(mN^2)$ arithmetic operations and $O(N)$ working memory. The shift $-m$ and constant coefficient $1$ are invariants that audit the calculation.

\subsection{Multiplicity recovery}
Given a full-column-rank matrix $X$, precompute a left inverse $L$ and return $m_n=La_n$. For a square $r\times r$ table, elimination costs $O(r^3)$ once and each new degree costs $O(r^2)$. Exact arithmetic permits checks of integrality and nonnegativity. If rank is deficient, a nonzero kernel vector $z$ certifies a collision between $m$ and $m+z$ whenever both are admissible.

\subsection{Transformation audit}
Determine a subgroup common to all factors, infer invariance of the product, and compare any claimed factor $J$ with $1$ at a nonzero point. A nontrivial $J$ demands a compensator. Its weight and divisor must then be tracked explicitly. This logical audit should precede numerical coefficient matching.

\section{Examples and applications}
Take
\[
R_1=1+q,\qquad R_2=1+2q,\qquad R_3=1-q+q^2.
\]
Then
\[
R_1R_2R_3=1+2q+q^3+2q^4
\]
and
\[
T_1T_2T_3=q^{-3}+2q^{-2}+2q^{-1}+1+2q.
\]
The pole order is exactly three.

For reconstruction, consider
\[
X=\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\1&-1\end{pmatrix},\qquad
m=\begin{pmatrix}3\\2\end{pmatrix},\qquad
Xm=\begin{pmatrix}5\\1\end{pmatrix}.
\]
Since $X^{-1}=\frac12X$, the traces recover $m$. If only the first row is retained, the map becomes $(x,y)\mapsto x+y$; the distinct vectors $(3,2)$ and $(4,1)$ both map to $5$.

This has a machine-learning interpretation. Multiplicities are latent variables, character values form a linear observation layer, and full column rank is identifiability. Kernel vectors are invisible perturbations. Integrality and positivity are priors that may restore uniqueness on a discrete cone. Selecting conjugacy classes becomes an experimental-design problem balancing rank and numerical conditioning. Multiplication is a separate feature aggregator: valuations add, but labels are generally lost.

\section{Analytic scope and future work}
A genuine moonshine product theorem must place all factors in a common analytic setting. Each factor should be meromorphic on the upper half-plane and at the cusps of a finite-index subgroup. When levels differ, one passes to an intersection subgroup and reconciles cusp widths and local parameters. Divisors then add under finite multiplication.

Four directions follow. First, construct all $194$ normalized McKay--Thompson functions compatibly and prove an exact pole of order $194$ at infinity. Second, find a canonical minimal-divisor compensator producing a prescribed positive weight. Third, determine the least coefficient threshold $N$ for injective recovery under integrality and positivity. Fourth, prove a general cross-level finite-product theorem computing every cusp divisor from the individual divisors. Numerical work should additionally study singular values of row-restricted character matrices, since algebraic injectivity alone does not guarantee stable decoding.

\section{Conclusion}
Finite normalized products obey three independent principles. Principal exponents add. Weight-zero invariance survives finite multiplication. Character information survives evaluation exactly at the injectivity boundary. Accordingly, multiplying $194$ class functions does not automatically create positive weight or preserve every label. A rigorous version of the claim that the Monster ``is a modular form'' must specify the indexing, common symmetry, compensator, divisor, and decoding map. The results above provide the algebraic bookkeeping and obstruction theory for that program.

\end{document}

